<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur III, Duke of Brittany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1393–<a href="/years/1458">1458</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Arthur de Richemont</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur III, known before his accession as Arthur de Richemont, was constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1425 and duke of Brittany from 1457 to 1458. A son of Duke John IV, he was wounded and captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> in 1415, later served Charles VII and reformed the royal army. He shared in the victories of Patay in 1429 and Formigny in 1450 and in the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1436.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-iii-duke-of-brittany.jpg" alt="Arthur III, Duke of Brittany" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armorial_de_Gilles_le_Bouvier_BNF_Fr4985_f17v.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
